Section~\ref{sec:failure} showed that action selection can depend on a small difference between two SMs even when their dominant structure is similar.
Standard FB approximates these SMs through a low-dimensional density-ratio factorization and point-wise squared regression.
A model can therefore fit most of the successor structure while poorly representing the smaller difference that determines the action gap in Equation~\eqref{eq:action-gap}.
\name{} mitigates this by changing three parts of FB.
First, we normalize the successor measure and model the resulting density as a probability distribution.
Second, we replace point-wise squared regression with a categorical Bellman objective.
Third, because $F^\top B$ now represents a score rather than the successor density itself, we compute action values by integrating rewards against the learned distribution.
We first describe the successor-learning objective (\cref{sec:method-learning}) and then the corresponding value extraction, actor update, and downstream reward inference (\cref{sec:method-control}).

\subsection{Learning a normalized successor distribution}
\label{sec:method-learning}

Recall that standard FB represents the successor density ratio using Equation~\eqref{eq:fb-factorization} and fits it with point-wise squared regression.
\name{} keeps the forward--backward factorization but changes what it represents and how it is fitted.

Every discounted successor measure has total mass $1/(1-\gamma)$.
We therefore define
\begin{equation}
    \nu^{\pi_z}(s,a,\cdot) := (1-\gamma)M^{\pi_z}(s,a,\cdot), \qquad \nu^{\pi_z}(s,a,\mathcal S) = 1. 
    \label{eq:normalized-sm}
\end{equation}
For any bounded reward $r$,
\begin{equation}
    \int_{\mathcal S} r(s^+) \nu^{\pi_z}(s,a,ds^+) = (1-\gamma)Q_r^{\pi_z}(s,a), 
    \label{eq:normalized-value-exact}
\end{equation}
so normalization does not change which action is preferred.
The main change in \name{} is to learn the normalized successor measure directly as a distribution.

The normalized SM satisfies the Bellman equation
\begin{equation}
\nu^\pi(s,a,\cdot) = (1-\gamma)P(\cdot\mid s,a) + \gamma \E_{\substack{ s'\sim P(\cdot\mid s,a)\ a'\sim\pi(\cdot\mid s')}} \left[ \nu^\pi(s',a',\cdot) \right]. \label{eq}
\end{equation}

For a task $z$ and its policy $\pi_z$, define the normalized successor density relative to $\rho$ as
\begin{equation}
    h^{\pi_z}(s,a,s^+) := \frac{ d\nu^{\pi_z}(s,a,\cdot) }{ d\rho }(s^+). 
    \label{eq:true-density}
\end{equation}

We retain the bilinear representation used by FB, but use its output as a score, normalized as
\begin{equation}
    h_\theta(s,a,z,s^+) := \frac{ \exp f_\theta(s,a,z,s^+) }{ \E_{\tilde s^+\sim\rho} \left[ \exp f_\theta(s,a,z,\tilde s^+) \right] }. 
    \label{eq:density-model}
\end{equation}
The exponential makes the prediction non-negative, while the denominator makes it integrate to one under $\rho$.
The normalizer can also be estimated using states already sampled from the offline dataset.

The normalized successor measure satisfies the Bellman equation
\begin{equation}
    \nu^{\pi_z}(s,a,\cdot) = (1-\gamma)P(\cdot\mid s,a) + \gamma \E_{\substack{ s'\sim P(\cdot\mid s,a)\\ a'\sim\pi_z(\cdot\mid s') }} \left[ \nu^{\pi_z}(s',a',\cdot) \right]. \label{eq:normalized-bellman}
\end{equation}
In practice, we represent this distribution using the next states in each minibatch.
For transitions $\{(s_i,a_i,s_i')\}_{i=1}^{n}$, define
\begin{equation}
    p_{ij} := \softmax_j \left( f_\theta(s_i,a_i,z_i,s_j') \right). 
    \label{eq:density-probabilities}
\end{equation}
Each row $p_i$ is therefore a probability distribution over the candidate future states ${s_j'}_{j=1}^{n}$.

We use the same states to construct the Bellman target.
Let $ a_i' \sim \pi_\omega(\cdot\mid s_i',z_i),$ and define
\begin{equation}
    \bar p_{ij}' := \softmax_j \left( \frac{ \bar F(s_i',a_i',z_i)^\top \bar B(s_j') }{ \tau } \right), 
    \label{eq:target-density-probabilities}
\end{equation}
where $\bar F$ and $\bar B$ are target networks.

With $ d_i := \gamma (1-\mathrm{terminal}i),$ the sampled Bellman target is
\begin{equation}
    t{ij} := \sg \left[ (1-d_i)\ind{j=i} + d_i\bar p_{ij}' \right]. 
    \label{eq}
\end{equation}
The observed next state gives the immediate part of the target, while $\bar p_i'$ gives the distribution over later states.

We fit this target with cross-entropy,
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal L_{\mathrm{DFB}} := - \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \sum_{j=1}^{n} t_{ij} \log p_{ij}. 
    \label{eq:density-loss}
\end{equation}

\begin{proposition}[Correct Bellman fixed point]
\label{prop:density-bellman}
Fix a task $z$ and its policy $\pi_z$.
At the population level, if the model can represent the Bellman target exactly, minimizing the cross-entropy in Equation~\eqref{eq:density-loss} performs one Bellman update of the normalized successor distribution.
Repeated exact updates converge to the normalized successor distribution of $\pi_z$.
\end{proposition}

The proof and technical assumptions are given \Cref{app:theory}.
The proposition shows that \name{} changes how the successor measure is fitted without changing the quantity recovered by exact Bellman updates.
It does not claim that \name{} must preserve action differences better than FB under limited function approximation; we test this experimentally in Section~\ref{sec:experiments}.

The squared and categorical objectives nevertheless weight approximation errors differently.
Because standard FB fits a density ratio relative to $\rho$, this difference depends on both the successor distribution and how frequently a state appears in the offline data.
Appendix~\ref{app:loss-geometry} shows that, for small relative errors, squared density-ratio regression weights an error proportionally to $\nu_j^2/\rho_j$, whereas the categorical objective weights it proportionally to $\nu_j$.
This motivates the change in objective without claiming that every rare future state is automatically preserved.

\subsection{Using the learned distribution for control}
\label{sec:method-control}

The normalized parameterization changes how action values are read from the representation.
In standard FB, the linear factorization gives the value readout in Equation~\eqref{eq:fb-value-readout}.
In \name{}, $F^\top B$ instead defines a score inside the normalized distribution in Equation~\eqref{eq:density-model}, so this mapping no longer applies.

For any reward $r$, we evaluate an action directly under the predicted successor distribution:
\begin{equation}
    \hat q_{\theta,r}(s,a,z) := \sum_{j=1}^{K} p_j(s,a,z)\, r(s_j^+), \qquad s_j^+\sim\rho. 
    \label{eq:density-value}
\end{equation}

During pretraining, a task vector $z$ defines the reward
\begin{equation}
    r_z(s) := B_\theta(s)^\top z. \label{eq:latent-reward}
\end{equation}
Using the mini-batch next states as candidate future states, we therefore compute
\begin{equation}
    \hat q_\theta(s,a,z) := \sum_{j=1}^{n} p_j(s,a,z)\, B_\theta(s_j')^\top z. 
    \label{eq:density-actor-value}
\end{equation}

The actor is trained to maximize this value while retaining the same offline regularization as the FB baseline:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal L_{\mathrm{actor}} := - \frac{ \E_{s,z} \left[ \hat q_\theta \left( s, \pi_\omega(s,z), z \right) \right] }{ s_q } 
                                    + \lambda_{\mathrm{BC}} \mathcal L_{\mathrm{offline}}(\omega), 
    \label{eq:density-actor-loss}
\end{equation}
where $s_q$ is a detached estimate of the value scale over the batch.
The successor networks are fixed during this update.

The normalized parameterization also breaks the standard FB identity used to map a downstream reward directly to a task vector.
We therefore infer the task vector from reward-labelled offline states using a separate reward encoder, described in Appendix~\ref{app:reward-inference}.
At test time, all networks remain frozen: we infer the task vector from the new reward and execute the corresponding policy without additional training or environment interaction.
Thus, relative to standard FB, \name{} changes the successor parameterization, its Bellman objective, and the value readout used to train the actor.
The policy architecture, offline regularizer, and pretraining task distribution remain unchanged.